 \end{figure}
\end{frame}

%------------------------------------------------
\begin{frame}{Comments on the implementation in PREMIA}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Running the code solve MOT problems in a financia context with lognormal marginal laws, both in dimension 1 and 2. 
  \item It is recommended to use recentered empirical measures to have better bounds: for example $\mu_I=\sum_{i=1}\delta_{X_i-\bar{X}+m}$ and $\nu_I=\sum_{i=1}\delta_{Y_i-\bar{Y}+m}$ with $\bar{X}=\frac 1 I \sum_{i=1}^I X_i$ and $\bar{Y}=\frac 1 I \sum_{i=1}^I Y_i$, or   $\mu_I=\sum_{i=1}\delta_{X_i-\bar{X}+\bar{Y}}$ and $\nu_I=\sum_{i=1}\delta_{Y_i}$ when the mean $m$ is unkown.
  \item Python (2.7)  code, use of GLPK. For dimension $d\ge 2$, to solve the linear quadratic problem we have got a more efficient implementation with COIN-OR (interfaced with NSP) using also sparse matrices. Change the boolean ``useGLPK'' to False to load precomputed points calculated with COIN-OR.
   \item{\bf Reference:} A. Alfonsi, J. Corbetta, B. Jourdain : Sampling of probability measures in the convex order and approximation of Martingale Optimal Transport problems. Septembre 2017. {\tt https://arxiv.org/pdf/1709.05287}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

